Project: Personal Household Expense Management & Analysis
Real-Life Problem Statement
Every household makes a large number of expenses every day. These may include Kirana and groceries, vegetables, milk, electricity, water, gas, mobile and internet bills, building maintenance, property taxes, medical and hospital expenses, transportation, petrol, restaurants, hotels, tourism, education, clothing, insurance, EMIs, entertainment, domestic help, gifts and many other expenses.
In many households, these expenses are recorded in notebooks, spreadsheets, bank statements or are simply remembered. As a result, family members may not have a clear understanding of:
•	Where their money is going
•	Which expense categories consume the most money
•	Which expenses are increasing
•	How much is spent every month
•	How much is spent on recurring expenses
•	Which expenses are essential and non-essential
•	Which payment methods are used most frequently
•	Which expenses need attention or control
You are required to develop a Personal Household Expense Management and Analysis System using PostgreSQL.
The objective is not merely to create tables and write SQL queries. You must use real household expense data for at least three completed months, analyze the data, identify meaningful patterns and provide practical recommendations based on your findings.
________________________________________
1. Collect Real Household Expense Data
Each student/team must collect at least three months of past household expense data.
For example:
April, May and June
The three months should preferably be consecutive completed months.
The data may be collected from:
•	Household records
•	Expense notebooks
•	Spreadsheets
•	Bills
•	Receipts
•	Bank statements
•	Other available household records
Students should collect only the information necessary for the project.
Suggested information
For each expense, collect:
•	Date
•	Expense category
•	Expense subcategory
•	Amount
•	Family member associated with the expense
•	Vendor/shop/service provider, where available
•	Payment method
•	Description
•	Recurring or non-recurring
•	Paid/Pending status, where applicable
Privacy and Security
Do not enter sensitive personal financial information into the database.
Students must not include:
•	Bank account numbers
•	Credit/debit card numbers
•	UPI IDs
•	Passwords
•	PINs
•	Account credentials
Names and vendor information may be anonymized where required. Amounts may also be approximated if necessary.
